							</field>
							<field>
								<name>TODET_EIRQ_EN</name>
								<description>TIM TODETx IRQ interrupt enable. Coding see bit 0.</description>
								<lsb>4</lsb>
								<msb>4</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>GLITCHDET_EIRQ_EN</name>
								<description>TIM GLITCHDETx IRQ interrupt enable. Coding see bit 0.</description>
								<lsb>5</lsb>
								<msb>5</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
						</fields>
					</register>
					<register>
						<name>TIMi_INP_VAL</name>
						<description>TIM0 Input Value Observation Register
 resetvalue={Application Reset:0x0}</description>
						<addressOffset>116</addressOffset>
						<size>32</size>
						<resetValue>0</resetValue>
						<resetMask>4294967295</resetMask>
						<fields>
							<field>
								<name>F_OUT</name>
								<description>Signals after TIM FLT unit</description>
								<lsb>0</lsb>
								<msb>7</msb>
								<access>read-only</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>F_IN</name>
								<description>Signals after INPSRC selection  before TIM FLT unit</description>
								<lsb>8</lsb>
								<msb>15</msb>
								<access>read-only</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>TIM_IN</name>
								<description>Signals after TIM input signal synchronization</description>
								<lsb>16</lsb>
								<msb>23</msb>
								<access>read-only</access>
							</field>
						</fields>
					</register>
					<register>
						<name>TIMi_IN_SRC</name>
						<description>TIM0 AUX IN Source Selection Register
 resetvalue={Application Reset:0x0}</description>
						<addressOffset>120</addressOffset>
						<size>32</size>
						<resetValue>0</resetValue>
						<resetMask>4294967295</resetMask>
						<fields>
							<field>
								<name>VAL_0</name>
								<description>Value to be fed to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   VAL x 1  defines        the state of the signal . Function depends on the combination of VAL x 1  and MODE x 1  see MODE 0        description. Any read access to a VAL x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>0</lsb>
								<msb>1</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>MODE_0</name>
								<description>Input source to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   MODE x 1  defines the state of the signal . Function table  MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   0           160   160   160   160 The input signal defined by bit field CICTRL of the TIM channel is used   160   160   160   160 as input source. MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   1    160   160   160   160 The signal TIM AUX IN of the TIM channel is used as input source. MODE x 1  1     160   160   160   160 The state VAL x 1  defines the input level for the TIM channel. Any read access to a MODE x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>2</lsb>
								<msb>3</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>VAL_1</name>
								<description>Value to be fed to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   VAL x 1  defines        the state of the signal . Function depends on the combination of VAL x 1  and MODE x 1  see MODE 0        description. Any read access to a VAL x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>4</lsb>
								<msb>5</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>MODE_1</name>
								<description>Input source to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   MODE x 1  defines the state of the signal . Function table  MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   0           160   160   160   160 The input signal defined by bit field CICTRL of the TIM channel is used   160   160   160   160 as input source. MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   1    160   160   160   160 The signal TIM AUX IN of the TIM channel is used as input source. MODE x 1  1     160   160   160   160 The state VAL x 1  defines the input level for the TIM channel. Any read access to a MODE x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>6</lsb>
								<msb>7</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>VAL_2</name>
								<description>Value to be fed to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   VAL x 1  defines        the state of the signal . Function depends on the combination of VAL x 1  and MODE x 1  see MODE 0        description. Any read access to a VAL x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>8</lsb>
								<msb>9</msb>
								<access>read-write</access>
							</field>
							<field>
								<name>MODE_2</name>
								<description>Input source to Channel 7. Multicore encoding in use   MODE x 1  defines the state of the signal . Function table  MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   0           160   160   160   160 The input signal defined by bit field CICTRL of the TIM channel is used   160   160   160   160 as input source. MODE x 1  0   VAL x 1   1    160   160   160   160 The signal TIM AUX IN of the TIM channel is used as input source. MODE x 1  1     160   160   160   160 The state VAL x 1  defines the input level for the TIM channel. Any read access to a MODE x bit field will        always result in a value 00 or 11 indicating current state. A        modification of the state is only performed with the values 01 and 10.        Writing the values 00 and 11 is always ignored.</description>
								<lsb>10</lsb>
								<msb>11</msb>
