 resetvalue={Application Reset:0x0}</description>
					<addressOffset>144</addressOffset>
					<size>32</size>
					<resetValue>0</resetValue>
					<resetMask>4294967295</resetMask>
					<fields>
						<field>
							<name>TS</name>
							<description>Time Stamp   TS. of the last sync pulse sent for channel x.</description>
							<lsb>0</lsb>
							<msb>23</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
					</fields>
				</register>
				<register>
					<name>RDS</name>
					<description>Receive Status Register
 resetvalue={Application Reset:0x0}</description>
					<addressOffset>176</addressOffset>
					<size>32</size>
					<resetValue>0</resetValue>
					<resetMask>4294967295</resetMask>
					<fields>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC0</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>0</lsb>
							<msb>0</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC1</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>1</lsb>
							<msb>1</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC2</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>2</lsb>
							<msb>2</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC3</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>3</lsb>
							<msb>3</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC4</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>4</lsb>
							<msb>4</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRC5</name>
							<description>XCRC   XCRC5. CRC of last Packet Frame.  XCRC0 is on bit position 0.</description>
							<lsb>5</lsb>
							<msb>5</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>XCRCI</name>
							<description>XCRC Error Flag   XCRCI. This bit is set if the CRC check on the enveloping Packet Frame fails including the case where XCRC check can not be performed  non recoverable frames  see CROSSREFERENCE .</description>
							<lsb>6</lsb>
							<msb>6</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>CRC0</name>
							<description>CRC   CRC2. of last frame. CRC0   Parity is on bit position 7. If Parity is used  CRC1 2 are always 0.</description>
							<lsb>7</lsb>
							<msb>7</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>CRC1</name>
							<description>CRC   CRC2. of last frame. CRC0   Parity is on bit position 7. If Parity is used  CRC1 2 are always 0.</description>
							<lsb>8</lsb>
							<msb>8</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>CRC2</name>
							<description>CRC   CRC2. of last frame. CRC0   Parity is on bit position 7. If Parity is used  CRC1 2 are always 0.</description>
							<lsb>9</lsb>
							<msb>9</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>CRCI</name>
							<description>CRC Error Flag   CRCI. This bit is set if the CRC or Parity check on the transported PSI5 frame fails.</description>
							<lsb>10</lsb>
							<msb>10</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>ERR0</name>
							<description>Error signalling Flag 0   ERR0. This bit represents the status of the error signalling flag Err0 in the enveloping Packet Frame.</description>
							<lsb>11</lsb>
							<msb>11</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>ERR1</name>
							<description>Error signalling Flag 1   ERR1. This bit represents the status of the error signalling flag Err1 in the enveloping Packet Frame.</description>
							<lsb>12</lsb>
							<msb>12</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>HDI</name>
							<description>Header Error Signalled Flag   HDI. This bit is set if at least one of the error signalling flags in the enveloping Packet Frame Err0 and Err1 is set.</description>
							<lsb>13</lsb>
							<msb>13</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>PE</name>
							<description>ASC Parity Error Flag   PE. This bit is set if the error flag signalling a parity error was set during reception of one of the ASC Bytes transporting this PSI5 frame.</description>
							<lsb>14</lsb>
							<msb>14</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>FE</name>
							<description>ASC Framing Error Flag   FE. This bit is set if the error flag signaling a framing error was set during reception of one of the ASC Bytes transporting this PSI5 frame.</description>
							<lsb>15</lsb>
							<msb>15</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>OE</name>
							<description>ASC Overrun Error Flag   OE. This bit is set if the error flag signaling an overrun error was set during reception of one of the ASC Bytes transporting this PSI5 frame.</description>
							<lsb>16</lsb>
							<msb>16</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>TEI</name>
							<description>Time Error Flag   TEI. This bit is set if the watch dog timer expired. Depending from RCRAx.WDMS either the distance between two RDIs is longer than specified in WDL or it expired without reception of CHCI in time. I.e. the time from issuing the sync pulse to reception of the last expected frame configured in NFC.NFx was too long. If WDMS is in synchronous mode  all frames after TEI have TEI set in RDS until either CHCI is issued or INSTATx.TEI is cleared by SW. RDS.TEI flag is independently from INTENx.TEI.</description>
							<lsb>17</lsb>
							<msb>17</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>RBI</name>
							<description>Receive Buffer Overflow Flag   RBI. This bit is set after a frame has been received while the old one was not read from RDR. I.e. the kernel wants to set interrupt RDI and finds RDI already set. The old data is overwritten by the new data.</description>
							<lsb>18</lsb>
							<msb>18</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>FID</name>
							<description>Frame ID  Frame Number    FID. See bit RCRAx.FIDS for actual content.</description>
							<lsb>19</lsb>
							<msb>21</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>CID</name>
							<description>Channel ID  Channel Number    CID</description>
							<lsb>22</lsb>
							<msb>24</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>AFC</name>
							<description>Actual UART Frame Count   AFC. This bit field shows the number of UART frames actually received. This is used to further analyze non recoverable frames by SW or during debugging.</description>
							<lsb>25</lsb>
							<msb>27</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
						<field>
							<name>PFC</name>
							<description>Packet Frame Count   PFC. For data consistency  RDR  RDS and TSM are tagged with a Packet Frame count value. For each channel there is a separate internal packet counter. This allows to read a triplet of RDR  RDS and TSM from the system memory without looking at interrupts. If the PFC is identical all three values belong together. Otherwise they need to be read again until PFC is identical.</description>
							<lsb>28</lsb>
							<msb>31</msb>
							<access>read-only</access>
						</field>
					</fields>
				</register>
